## What was actually broken, and it was not the CLI

The Bot the `default` profile resolves to carried a per-Bot model override of
`z-ai/glm-5.3-flash`. Measured against the gateway today, that id answers

    {\"code\": \"external_model_requires_payment\"}

as does its configured fallback `qwen/qwen3.8-flash`. So every turn on that Bot
failed the same way:

    provider/fallback-failed —
    z-ai/glm-5.3-flash (provider/empty-response)
      -> qwen/qwen3.8-flash (provider/model-mismatch)

`tool-count: 0`. Nothing reached a tool, because nothing reached a model.

**33 of 231 Bots** carried the same kind of pin (21 on the GLM id, 12 on the
Qwen one). The other 198 were already on `murakumo-main`, which is what
CLAUDE.md's model-alias rule says to use and the only id in the gateway
registry that answers at all: of the nine listed, `murakumo-main` returns text,
`murakumo-edge` returns HTTP 200 with empty content, and the rest are
unreachable or payment-gated.

All 33 were repointed at `murakumo-main` through `itonami bots model`, and the
result was read back FROM THE SERVER rather than from the loop's own log: 229
on `murakumo-main`, 0 on a failing id.

## Tool calls work

Measured after the repoint, on the same Bot, twice:

- `workspace_list` on `manifest/`: `tool-count 1`, `turn-count 2`, completed in
  38s, and the answer named the first three real entries.
- The reported question, re-run: **8 tool calls executed**, then the turn ended
  at `turn-budget-exhausted`.

The second is not a tool defect. That Bot's whole tool set is
`workspace_list workspace_search git_log workspace_read git_status git_diff` --
six filesystem and git tools -- and the question is about Bot profiles, which
none of them can read. It searched the workspace until its budget ran out. The
question is answerable, but not by that Bot: `/bots`, `/profiles` and
`itonami bots list` answer it directly.

## The answer to the question itself

There is no 人材/HR Bot profile in the fleet: 0 of 231 Bots match 人材 / 採用 /
人事 / talent / recruit / HR across name, role, section, brief and
responsibilities. The workforce roles that DO exist are QA (19), Engineer (19),
Business Owner (19), Product Manager (17), Customer Support (9), Financial
Chief (9), Marketer (8), Sales (6), Product Designer (6), Kaizen analyst (3),
and a tail of per-title content Bots.

The workspace does carry `cloud-itonami/talent` and `cloud-itonami/recruit`
repositories. Neither has a Bot.

## Why the session died, and why it no longer does

The turn failed on the stream, `finish-run!` saw a non-completed status, and
the build the operator was running called `js/process.exit exit-refused` there.
The session ended on the server's answer to one message.

That was already changed on 2026-09-07 (ADR-2609071400): in the REPL a refusal
ends the TURN, and `itonami chat \"…\"` keeps `exit 1`, which is where a status
has to be readable by a script. Verified here by occupying the Bot from outside
the CLI and then sending a message: the refusal prints and `/status` is
answered afterwards, which it could not be if the process had exited.

Two things were wrong with how it printed, and are fixed:

- **It said the same sentence twice** -- once from the stream handler and once
  from the outcome reporter. The error is carried out on `:error` and reported
  once, by whoever owns the outcome.
- **It did not say what to do.** `/stop` is not the answer when the run belongs
  to somebody else, so the hint names `/bots` and `/profile <id>` instead.

## What is still true and worth knowing

`:model-fallbacks` in the operator config reads

    murakumo-main      -> z-ai/glm-5.3-flash
    z-ai/glm-5.3-flash -> qwen/qwen3.8-flash
    qwen/qwen3.8-flash -> murakumo-main

Every hop but the last is payment-gated today, so the \"one explicit GLM
fallback\" its own comment describes is dead: a transient `murakumo-main`
hiccup becomes a hard failure with a two-model error message that reads as
though two things were tried.

It was NOT changed here, because there is nothing live to point it at -- of the
whole registry only `murakumo-main` answers. Removing the entry would improve
the diagnosis (one clear cause instead of a misleading chain) without improving
the success rate. That is an operator decision about their own config, and it
is recorded rather than made.

The 143 Bots carrying a 15-minute resident job can still collide with an
interactive turn on the same Bot. `default` is not one of them, so it is not
what the operator hit -- what they hit was more likely this session's own test
runs, which is worth saying plainly.
